.TH DHCPRELAYD.CONF 5 "November 2026" "dhcprelayd" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
dhcprelayd.conf \- configuration file for the dhcprelayd DHCP relay agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/dhcprelayd.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR dhcprelayd (8)
reads its configuration from this file at startup, on SIGHUP, and when
interfaces or their addresses change.  The file has
.B key = value
settings, and sections with a keyword, a name, and a body in braces:
.PP
.RS
.nf
server 192.168.2.1 {
    port = 1067
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
An empty section is written
.BR {} .
Comments start with
.BR # .
A text value with spaces, or with one of
.B #={}(),+
in it, must be quoted, e.g.
.BR "circuit-id = \(dqcabinet 3\(dq" .
.PP
The file is checked when it is read.  An error, e.g. an unknown setting
or an invalid address, is logged with the file name and line number, and
the file is rejected.
.SH GLOBAL SETTINGS
.TP
.BI server " address " { " ... " }
A DHCP server to relay requests to.  Requests are sent to all servers.
At least one server is required, here or in each
.B interface
section.  Replies are relayed only from these addresses, so use the
address the server replies from.  For a DHCP server on the same system
use 127.0.0.1, a gateway address is rejected, see
.BR dhcprelayd (8).
.RS
.TP
.BI port " = port"
UDP port of the server, default 67.
.RE
.TP
.BI force-server-identity " = true" \fR|\fB false
Add the server identifier override sub-option from RFC 5107 to option 82,
with the gateway address.  The server then gives the gateway address as
its server identifier, so clients send their renewals to the relay agent
instead of to the server directly.  The server must support the
sub-option.  Takes effect only with an
.B option82
section.  Default false.
.TP
.BI udp-listen-port " = port"
Also receive requests and server replies on this UDP port, e.g. from
relay agents or servers that use a port other than 67.  Default 67, no
extra port.
.TP
.BR option82 " { " ... " }"
Add the relay agent information option, option 82, to each request, and
remove it from replies.  Without this section the option is not added.
.RS
.TP
.BI policy " = " replace \fR|\fB keep \fR|\fB drop
What to do with a request from a client that already carries option 82.
A client should never send it, it is meant to be added by a relay agent.
Does not apply to interfaces and ports set as
.BR trusted ,
there option 82 is always kept.
.RS
.TP
.B replace
Remove the client's option 82 and add the relay agent's own, so a client
cannot pick its own circuit-id.  The default.
.TP
.B keep
Forward the request with the client's option 82, on all interfaces and
ports.
.TP
.B drop
Discard the request.
.RE
.TP
.BI remote-id-type " = " hostname \fR|\fB giaddr
Use the hostname, the default, or the gateway address of the interface
as text, as remote-id.
.TP
.BI remote-id " = text"
Use a fixed text as remote-id.
.TP
.BI remote-id-hex " = xx:xx:..."
Use fixed bytes as remote-id, see
.BR "HEX VALUES" .
.PP
Set at most one of
.BR remote-id-type ,
.BR remote-id ,
and
.BR remote-id-hex .
.RE
.TP
.BI interface " name " { " ... " }
Relay requests received on this interface.  At least one is required.
The interface need not exist when the file is read, requests are relayed
from it when it shows up, and with
.B giaddr auto
when it has an IPv4 address.  The same goes for its ports.
.RS
.TP
.BI giaddr " = " auto \fR|\fI address
Gateway address, sent to the server and used by the server to select the
subnet.  An IPv4 address, or
.BR auto ,
the default, for the first IPv4 address of the interface.
.TP
.BI option82 " = true" \fR|\fB false
Set to false to relay requests from this interface without adding option
82.  Option 82 in a request is then kept, whatever the
.BR policy .
Default true.  Has no effect without the
.B option82
section.
.TP
.BI server " address " { " ... " }
Servers for this interface, same syntax as the global
.BR server .
They replace the global servers for this interface.
.TP
.BI trusted " = true" \fR|\fB false
Set to true where another relay agent, or a switch that adds option 82,
is connected.  Option 82 in a request is then kept, whatever the
.BR policy ,
and requests that are already relayed by another relay agent, with
giaddr set, are forwarded unchanged.  The client hardware address in a
request is not checked against the Ethernet source address of the frame.
A client could send such requests too, so set it only where needed.  Applies to all ports of the interface
unless set in a
.B port
section.  Default false: the
.B policy
applies, and already relayed requests are dropped.
.TP
.BI port " name " { " ... " }
A bridge port to relay requests from, when the interface is a bridge.
Only the listed ports are relayed from.  Without
.B port
sections, requests are relayed from the interface itself.
.RS
.TP
.BI circuit-id " = text"
Circuit-id in option 82, default the name of the port.
.TP
.BI circuit-id-hex " = xx:xx:..."
Circuit-id as bytes, see
.BR "HEX VALUES" .
.TP
.BI tagged " = true" \fR|\fB false
Set to true for a port that is a tagged member of the VLAN, e.g. a trunk
port.  Only requests tagged with the VLAN of the interface are then
relayed, so the port can be listed in more than one
.B interface
section, one for each of its VLANs.  The interface must be a VLAN
interface.  Default false: only untagged requests are relayed, and the
port can be listed in one
.B interface
section.
.TP
.BI trusted " = true" \fR|\fB false
As
.B trusted
for the interface, for this port only.
.PP
Set
.B circuit-id
or
.BR circuit-id-hex ,
not both.
.RE
.RE
.SH HEX VALUES
The
.B -hex
settings take colon separated pairs of hex digits, e.g.
.BR c0:ff:ee ,
for values that are not text, including NUL bytes.  Text and hex values
are 1 to 50 bytes.
.SH EXAMPLES
Relay requests on
.B eth1
to the DHCP server at 198.19.10.2, with the first IPv4 address of
.B eth1
as gateway address:
.PP
.RS
.nf
server 198.19.10.2 {}

interface eth1 {}
.fi
.RE
.PP
IP address per port on a VLAN filtering bridge.  Requests from bridge
ports eth0 and eth6 in VLAN 1 are relayed to 192.168.100.2, and requests
from eth3 in VLAN 2 to the global server, with option 82:
.PP
.RS
.nf
server 192.168.2.1 {}

option82 {
    remote-id = switch10
}

interface vlan1 {
    giaddr = 192.168.1.6
    server 192.168.100.2 {}
    port eth0 {}
    port eth6 {
        circuit-id = cabinet-3
    }
}

interface vlan2 {
    port eth3 {
        circuit-id-hex = 00:03
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/dhcprelayd.conf
Default configuration file, another file is given with
.B \-f
to
.BR dhcprelayd (8).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dhcprelayd (8)
.PP
RFC 3046, DHCP Relay Agent Information Option.
.br
RFC 5107, DHCP Server Identifier Override Suboption.
